<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gloria in excelsis Deo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">2nd or 3rd century; Latin translation 4th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Gloria in excelsis Deo&quot; (&quot;Glory to God in the highest&quot;) is a Christian hymn also known as the Greater Doxology, the Angelic Hymn or the Hymn of the Angels. It opens with the words the angels sing to the shepherds at the birth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospel-of-luke">Gospel of Luke</a>, and other verses were added very early to make a hymn of praise. The scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> Flusser linked the verse in Luke with ancient Jewish liturgy.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The hymn is an example of the psalmi idiotici, private psalms composed by individuals in imitation of the biblical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-psalms">Psalter</a>, which were popular in the 2nd and 3rd centuries. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/te-deum">Te Deum</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phos-hilaron">Phos Hilaron</a> are other surviving examples. In the 4th century it became part of morning prayers, and it is still recited in the Byzantine Rite Orthros service. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> translation is traditionally attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hilary-of-poitiers">Hilary of Poitiers</a>, who may have learned it in the East in 359–360.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> the hymn renders the Greek hypsístois of Luke 2:14 as excelsis, not altissimis, the word <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerome">Jerome</a> preferred in his translation. The Vulgate was commissioned only in 382. The Latin text praises God in a series of verbs (we praise you, we bless you, we adore you, we glorify you), addresses Jesus Christ as the Lamb of God who takes away the sins of the world, and closes with the words that he alone is the Holy One, the Lord and the Most High.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>In the Byzantine Rite it is the Greater Doxology, always sung, at Orthros on Sundays and feast days. The priest exclaims &quot;Glory to Thee, Who hast shown us the Light&quot;, the choir begins, and all the lamps and candles are lit. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coptic-orthodox-church">Coptic Orthodox Church</a> it is a congregational prayer, called the Praise of the Angels, said at the Morning Raising of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">Incense</a>. The Syriac Orthodox Church knows it as Teshbuhto d'malakhe.</p><p>In the Roman Rite it is sung or said at Mass after the Kyrie on Sundays outside Lent and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/advent">Advent</a>, and on solemnities and feasts. A tradition recorded in the Liber Pontificalis ascribes its use at the Christmas Mass to Pope Telesphorus and its use on Sundays and martyrs' feasts, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> only, to Pope Symmachus (498–514). Priests were allowed it at first only at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter">Easter</a> and on their ordination day, but by the end of the 11th century they used it on Sundays and feasts. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a>'s 1549 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-common-prayer">Book of Common Prayer</a> it stood in the Roman position, but it was later moved to the end of the service.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Gloria has been sung to a wide variety of melodies, and modern scholars have catalogued well over two hundred used in the medieval church. Almost all polyphonic settings of the Mass include it, and there are settings of the Gloria alone by J. S. Bach (BWV 191), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-vivaldi">Antonio Vivaldi</a> (RV 589 and RV 588), Francis Poulenc and others. The German paraphrase Allein Gott in der Höh sei Ehr was an early Lutheran hymn, and the Christmas carol Angels We Have Heard on High uses its beginning as a refrain.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gloria in excelsis Deo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
